; xhci98.inf - xHCI USB host controller miniport
;
; One file, two setup engines, one xhci98.sys. Windows 98 SE's 16-bit engine
; reads only the undecorated sections and loads the driver through
; *NTKERN/NTMPDriver; Windows 2000 SP4 prefers the .NTx86-decorated sections
; and loads it through a kernel service. Both install sections copy this
; project's two files through the shared Xhci.CopyFiles, so that list cannot
; drift between targets, and each adds its own OS-supplied list (Xhci.CopyW98,
; Xhci.CopyNT) for the files LayoutFile has Windows fetch from its own source.
;
; usbport.sys gets no service and no registry entry on either target: it loads
; because xhci98.sys imports USBPORT_RegisterUSBPortDriver from it, and the
; loader resolves that at image load. On Win98 the USB 2.0 stack (NUSB, or
; SweetLow's) has already placed it in SYSTEM32\DRIVERS, and the 9x path does
; not copy it. On the NT targets it is the OS's own file, and an NT install
; that never had a USB controller does not have it (Code 39, measured on
; Windows XP on 2026-09-03), so the NT path copies it the way the two files
; below are copied. Windows ME reads the undecorated half too (the same setup
; engine) and needs SweetLow's USB 2.0 stack first: its stock USB stack has
; no usbport.sys, so on a stock Windows ME machine the driver installs and
; NTKERN reports Code 2. Windows ME is supported in virtual machines only
; (docs\contributing\build-and-test.md, "Windows ME target VM"). 32-bit
; Windows XP reads the .NTx86 half like Windows 2000 and is supported in
; virtual machines only as well, since 1.0.1.0 (the same document, "Windows
; XP target VM"). 32-bit Windows Vista and Windows 7 take a third path, through
; the NT 6.x models section below, which copies this project's binary alone.
;
; usbd.sys and usbhub.sys ARE copied on both paths, usbui.dll on all four
; since 1.0.2.0, and none of the four files travels on this media. All are
; the operating system's own, and
; nothing on an xHCI-only machine ever places them: Windows setup copies its
; USB driver files only when it recognises a USB controller, and an xHCI-only
; machine looks empty to it. usbhub20.sys imports USBD.SYS on both targets,
; so without usbd.sys the USB 2.0 root hub cannot load; usbhub.sys is Windows
; 98's composite parent and the NT targets' hub driver. So this INF asks the
; setup engine to copy each from the OS's own install source (LayoutFile=
; below), with flag 16 so a file already on the machine is never touched. See
; docs\contributing\build-and-test.md, "The files the OS supplies:
; usbport.sys, usbd.sys, usbhub.sys and usbui.dll". Release 1.0.0.0 carried
; usbd.sys and usbhub.sys on the media instead, under per-target names; that
; was withdrawn before any upload.
;
; Both halves are transcribed from the shipping reference that installs the
; equivalent EHCI miniport on that target:
;   9x half - NUSB 3.3 USB2.INF, sections [EHCI] / [EHCI.AddReg]
;   NT half - Windows 2000 SP4 I386\USB.IN_, sections [EHCI.NT] /
;             [EHCI.NT.Services] / [EHCI.AddService]
; See docs\contributing\build-and-test.md, "The INF must carry both install
; paths" and "Win98 INF-parser traps". scripts\inf-gate\check-inf.ps1 enforces
; the rules this file is built to satisfy.

[Version]
Signature="$CHICAGO$"
Class=USB
ClassGUID={36FC9E60-C465-11CF-8056-444553540000}
Provider=%Provider%
; LayoutFile is what lets the two OS files below come from the OS. A CopyFiles
; entry whose file this INF's own [SourceDisksFiles] does not name is resolved
; through the OS's layout.inf instead (C:\WINDOWS\INF\LAYOUT.INF on Windows 98,
; %SystemRoot%\inf\layout.inf on Windows 2000), which records the cab each
; file lives in, and the engine fetches it from the Windows source path: the
; CABs on the hard disk (C:\WINDOWS\OPTIONS\CABS) or the Windows 98 CD, and
; Windows 2000's own Driver Cache\i386\driver.cab. This is how the OS's own
; INFs get their files, and Windows ME's USB.INF spells the same directive as
; "LayoutFile=Layout.inf, Layout1.inf, Layout2.inf".
LayoutFile=layout.inf
; DriverVer is not cosmetic on Windows 2000: the setup engine ranks candidate
; drivers by date-then-version, so a package whose version does not exceed the
; installed one is declined as not-better and an upgrade over an older build
; cannot pass. SP4's own [EHCI.NT] carries "DriverVer=2/15/2003,5.1.2600.1"
; with the comment "Date and Ver should be > any preSP4 QFE".
;
; The scheme: one four-part version per released package, bumped in the last
; field, with the date set to the release date and never moving backwards. It
; is a package version, not a build counter. The major version says whether
; the package is a final release (0.x were pre-releases).
; docs\contributing\build-and-test.md, "Versioning the driver", has the
; procedure.
;
; It must equal src\xhci_version.h, which is the one place the version and the
; release date are edited. scripts\inf-gate\check-inf.ps1 refuses the two
; disagreeing, and scripts\package\make-package.ps1 checks the version compiled
; into the .sys it stages against this line.
DriverVer=09/18/2026,1.1.0.0

; Provider and the manufacturer are two different fields. [Version]'s Provider
; is who supplied the driver (Driver tab, "Driver Provider"); the key below is
; the manufacturer of the device as this driver reports it (General tab,
; "Manufacturer", stored as Mfg). The first is a person and the second is the
; project: this driver binds by class code, so the silicon is Intel's or AMD's
; or Renesas's and naming anyone here as its maker would be false.
;
; The one TargetOSVersion field, NTx86.6.0, selects [XhciModels.NTx86.6.0] on
; Windows Vista and Windows 7 and nothing anywhere else. This is the line Windows
; 98's 16-bit engine parses to find its models section, so it stayed undecorated
; until the field was read on every engine that parses this file (2026-09-16,
; roadmap task 22.5): Windows 98 SE and Windows ME offered and installed the
; driver from [XhciModels], and Windows 2000 SP4 and 32-bit XP ran
; [Xhci.Dev.NTx86] from it, each off a snapshot that had never had this driver.
; Add no other field: nothing else has been read on those engines, and
; "NTx86,NTamd64" is the single-INF merge design record 11's decision 2
; declined. scripts\inf-gate\check-inf.ps1 refuses both (PATH-MFGDEC).
[Manufacturer]
%Mfg%=XhciModels,NTx86.6.0

; PCI\CC_0C0330 is the xHCI class-code compatible ID, the exact analog of the
; PCI\CC_0C0320 catch-all both reference INFs use for EHCI. Nothing else on
; either target claims it (the device sits unclaimed at Code 28 on Windows 98
; and Code 1 on Windows 2000), so a compatible-ID match needs no
; vendor-specific entry to outrank.
[XhciModels]
%XhciDesc%=Xhci.Dev,PCI\CC_0C0330

; A different install section NAME rather than a .6.0 decoration on Xhci.Dev:
; the version decoration selects the models section only, and the install
; section it names takes the platform extension and nothing further, so a
; [Xhci.Dev.NTx86.6.0] would never be looked for (measured on Vista x64
; 2026-09-10; docs\contributing\lessons.md, "A version-decorated install
; section is silently never read").
[XhciModels.NTx86.6.0]
%XhciDesc%=Xhci.Dev6,PCI\CC_0C0330

[SourceDisksNames]
1=%DiskName%,xhci98.sys,,

; Only this project's two files are on the media. usbport.sys, usbd.sys and
; usbhub.sys are deliberately NOT listed: a file named here is fetched from
; this disk, and a file not named here is fetched through LayoutFile from
; the OS's own source.
[SourceDisksFiles]
xhci98.sys=1
xhci98.inf=1

; Dirid 10 + System32\Drivers, never dirid 12: 12 resolves to
; \Windows\System\Iosubsys on Win98 and would install the driver to the wrong
; directory with no error. %12% appears once below, in ServiceBinary, inside a
; section Win98 never reads.
;
; Dirid 11 is the one exception, and it is not a driver: usbui.dll is a
; user-mode property-page DLL. 11 resolves to \Windows\System on Windows 98
; and Windows ME and to \WINNT\system32 (or \Windows\system32) on the NT
; targets, which is where all four operating systems' own USB INFs put it -
; USBUI.CopyFiles=11 on 9x, USBUI.CopyFiles.NT=11 on NT. It therefore needs
; its own CopyFiles section: it cannot ride Xhci.CopyW98 or Xhci.CopyNT,
; which are both 10,System32\Drivers.
[DestinationDirs]
DefaultDestDir=10
Xhci.CopyFiles=10,System32\Drivers
Xhci.CopyW98=10,System32\Drivers
Xhci.CopyNT=10,System32\Drivers
Xhci.CopyUI=11
Inf.CopyFiles=17

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Windows 98 SE (undecorated - Win98 ignores decorated section names)
; ------------------------------------------------------------------

[Xhci.Dev]
AddReg=Xhci.AddReg
CopyFiles=Xhci.CopyFiles,Xhci.CopyW98,Xhci.CopyUI

; The device's whole 9x registry footprint. The EnumPropPages value is the
; third one NUSB's [EHCI.AddReg] writes, transcribed from it like the two
; above; it is cosmetic, affecting no binding and no load, and it is the 9x
; half alone. The NT counterparts, EnumPropPages32 and Controller, are written
; too since roadmap task 23.1.5 - in [Xhci.AddReg.NT], where the block above
; that section records what each NT guest showed.
;
; What it buys was MEASURED on 2026-09-07 in a Windows 98 guest rather than
; assumed: an Advanced tab appears between General and Driver, carrying a
; "Disable USB error detection" box and a Bandwidth Usage dialog that
; enumerates the bus. The provider that draws the TAB is sysclass.dll - which
; this INF does not copy and which every 9x machine already has, the owner's
; E460 included - so the 9x tab is one registry line and not a file question.
; GitHub issue 4 item 5 asked for the line; roadmap task 23.1 is where it
; landed.
;
; The DIALOG behind the button is a different matter, and an earlier version
; of this comment had it wrong. It said the tab and the dialog both rendered
; with usbui.dll renamed away. The tab does; the dialog does NOT. Measured
; 2026-09-20 on a Windows 98 SE guest under SweetLow's stack (roadmap task
; 23.1, Leg C, A4): with C:\WINDOWS\SYSTEM\USBUI.DLL renamed away in MS-DOS
; mode and the machine cold booted, the Advanced tab and its checkbox render
; unchanged, while Bandwidth Usage raises "Data Access Error - An error
; occurred while trying to access the requested data." So does the USB 2.0
; Root Hub's own Power properties button, which SweetLow's USB2.INF registers
; and which has nothing to do with this package - two independent witnesses.
; That is the shape sysclass.dll's strings predict: it is a 16-bit NE module
; carrying usbui.dll, USBControllerBandwidthPage, USBHubPowerPage and that
; error text adjacent, the LoadLibrary32W/GetProcAddress32W route by which a
; 16-bit module calls a 32-bit one, with its failure message.
;
; So usbui.dll, copied below, is NOT cosmetic on 9x: without it the tab has a
; button that cannot open. It has been copied on every install path since
; 1.0.2.0 and that does not change - only the reason does.
;
; What the checkbox WRITES, and why this driver cannot be harmed by it. Read
; STATICALLY on 2026-09-20 (strings out of the shipping binaries; nothing
; executed): sysclass.dll is a 16-bit NE module carrying the value name
; ErrorCheckingEnabled beside the key
; SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Usb and the string
; SystemTray_Main. That name is in NO USB driver this repository has
; extracted - not NUSB 3.3's or 3.6's usbport.sys/usbhub20.sys, not
; SweetLow's, not Windows 98 SE's own usbd.sys/usbhub.sys, not Windows 2000
; SP4's or Windows XP SP3's - and in exactly one file: SYSTRAY.EXE, the
; shell's system tray, which carries it beside usbui.dll,
; USBErrorMessagesEnable and \\.\HCD%d. So the box is a SHELL switch and not
; a stack one: it turns off the tray's own USB error reporting, which runs on
; every 9x machine with or without this value and with or without this tab.
; It can only quiet what already touches this controller, never add to it;
; and if any of that traffic does reach the miniport it arrives through
; usbport's PassThru, which xhciPassThru GUID-matches and answers
; MP_STATUS_NOT_SUPPORTED (src\xhci_dispatch.c). Two things about it are
; worth knowing anyway: the key is machine-wide, so a user who ticks the box
; changes every usbport controller and the value outlives this devnode -
; exactly the shape that got DisableSelectiveSuspend removed at 1.1.0.0, the
; difference being that this package writes nothing there and only exposes a
; box Windows already has.
;
; Windows ME reads this same undecorated section and its sysclass.dll is the
; same module: 27,408 B against Windows 98 SE's 27,184 B, and every extracted
; string identical bar one code fragment and the version resource (read
; statically the same day out of vm\winme.img and vm\win98.img).
[Xhci.AddReg]
HKR,,DevLoader,,*NTKERN
HKR,,NTMPDriver,,xhci98.sys
HKR,,EnumPropPages,,"sysclass.dll,USBControllerPropPage"
HKR,,XhciLogVerbosity,0x00010001,0
HKR,,XhciLogDebugView,0x00010001,0

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Idle suspend: this INF writes NO registry value for it any more
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
;
; Releases 1.0.0.0 to 1.0.2.0 wrote
;   HKLM,System\CurrentControlSet\Services\USB,DisableSelectiveSuspend,...,1
; from a [Xhci.AddReg.Global] section carried by all four install paths. Since
; 1.1.0.0 the driver declares USB_MINIPORT_FLAGS_DISABLE_SS (0x20) in its
; MiniPortFlags instead, and this file writes nothing outside the devnode.
;
; The problem is unchanged: usbport idle-suspends the controller when the bus
; goes quiet - the Windows 98 builds (NUSB's and SweetLow's alike) within about
; half a second of the last transfer, Windows XP's within about thirty seconds
; of a start with nothing attached, Windows 7's about nine seconds after the
; start - and a halted xHC cannot report a port change, so a device attached
; afterwards is seen by nothing until the user presses Refresh in Device
; Manager. The spec gates Port Status Change Event generation on HCHalted = '0'
; (p.322) and says EINT and PCD generate no interrupt (p.399), so unlike EHCI,
; which re-arms USBINTR.PCD across its halt, there is no interrupt this driver
; could leave enabled: the fix has to stop the suspend, not wake from it.
;
; Why the flag replaced the value. Three reasons, in the order they decided it:
;
;  1. The value was machine-wide. Services\USB is not the devnode's key, so it
;     changed behaviour for every controller usbport drives - Microsoft's EHCI
;     included - and it outlived the devnode, so removing the device did not
;     take it with it and this package could not know whether something else
;     wanted it. The flag names this controller and nothing else.
;  2. The per-controller registry values were the obvious replacement and they
;     do not survive. HcDisableSelectiveSuspend (9x, NT 5.x) and Windows 7's
;     HcDisableAllSelectiveSuspend both work when read, but usbport's own
;     USBPORTBUSIF_ControllerSelectiveSuspend writes HcDisableSelectiveSuspend
;     back, and Vista's usbhub calls it from a power-setting callback. Measured
;     on a Vista x86 guest 2026-09-17: setting the Balanced plan's USB
;     selective suspend to Enabled rewrote the value to 0 and suspended the
;     controller at once. Balanced defaults that setting to Enabled on battery.
;     The flag is outside that setter's reach.
;  3. Vista and Windows 7 share [Xhci.Dev6.NTx86] but not the value's name, so
;     the registry route needed two names on one path. The flag is one bit on
;     all nine usbport builds this driver runs under.
;
; What it costs: the controller never idles, which costs a little power, and
; unlike the value there is now no way for a user to turn it back on. The value
; offered no such knob in practice either - usbport refuses the root hub's
; "Allow the computer to turn off this device" while it is set, exactly as it
; refuses it for the flag. src\xhci_dispatch.c carries the addresses the flag
; was read at on each build, and
; docs\issues\05-idle-suspend-and-disableselectivesuspend.md is the record.
;
; Upgrades from 1.0.0.0 to 1.0.2.0 keep the old machine-wide value: it is
; outside the devnode, so nothing here removes it, and no DelReg is written for
; it on purpose - it would delete a value a user or another controller may want.
; It is harmless beside the flag (both produce the same state) and
; docs\using\release-notes.md says how to delete it by hand.

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The two log values, on both install paths
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
;
;   XhciLogVerbosity  the 0-4 ladder, and the WHOLE switch:
;                       0  off outright - the PassThru read channel answers
;                          exactly MP_STATUS_NOT_SUPPORTED and nothing can be
;                          read off the machine.  The default.
;                       1  the channel engaged, counter block only; ring off
;                       2  + the note ring - this is the log channel
;                       3  + the PORTSC table in XHCISNAP's companion text
;                       4  + kernel-address records in the note ring, which
;                          the companion text then prints; the bench level.
;                          The raw dump (.BIN extension, .PSC PORTSC) is
;                          whole at every level from 1 up; only the ring's
;                          content and the companion text are gated.
;                     The channel is present in EVERY build flavour; what the
;                     flavour decides is how much there is to read.
;   XhciLogDebugView  an EMISSION switch and nothing else: it hands the ring to
;                     DbgPrint at the PASSIVE flush.
;
; Both default to 0: the safe configuration is the default and the convenience
; is the thing asked for by name. An absent value is 0 too, and is not an
; error. An out-of-range XhciLogVerbosity is refused, not clamped: the driver
; falls back to 0 and records that it did, so a refusal shuts the channel
; rather than opening it at some other rung.
;
; The driver reads both through usbport's own UsbPortGetMiniportRegistryKeyValue
; service with BOOL = TRUE, which is IoOpenDeviceRegistryKey's
; PLUGPLAY_REGKEY_DRIVER, the software (driver) key: what a plain AddReg under
; an install section writes, and where the shipping usbehci.sys reads its own
; parameter from (docs\usb-xhci-info\usbport-miniport-abi.md section 6). There
; is no Set counterpart, so nothing here is ever written back by the driver;
; XHCISNAP sets these from user mode, and that is the documented way to turn
; the log on (enable, restart, reproduce, XHCISNAP -o C:\NAME). A ready-made
; .reg cannot ship because the software key is per machine.
;
; Both are written on BOTH paths: a value present on only one install path is
; exactly the failure shape the INF gate exists to catch, and
; scripts\inf-gate\check-inf.ps1 checks the 9x and the NT section separately
; for each of them. 0x00010001 is FLG_ADDREG_TYPE_DWORD; it is not an NT-only
; spelling, NUSB's own USBSTOR.INF writes "HKR,,DriverFlags,0x00010001,..."
; from an undecorated section.
;
; The enable step is also the access control. This driver cannot tighten the
; door it answers through: usbport owns the device object, hardcodes
; \DosDevices\HCD<n>, completes IRP_MJ_CREATE with no work, and the IOCTL is
; FILE_ANY_ACCESS. So while the channel is enabled, any local user who can open
; that name can read this driver's diagnostic state (counters, ring notes,
; PORTSC, kernel addresses); no user data and no other process's memory.
; Writing this value needs Administrators on Windows 2000 and nothing at all
; on Windows 98. Run XHCISNAP -disable after sending a capture.
;
; The cost of the defaults: the ring costs 16 KB of the miniport extension
; whatever this says; what the default saves is the append sites' cost at real
; interrupt rates on Windows 98 metal, which is unmeasured. An end user who has
; not asked for a log has no channel open at all.

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The NT half of the property page: TAKEN 2026-09-20, on every NT target
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
;
; The counterpart of the 9x EnumPropPages line above is a PAIR, and it is the
; pair all three NT references write for their own EHCI controller: Windows
; 2000 SP4's USB.INF [EHCI.AddReg.NT], Windows XP SP3's usbport.inf, and
; Windows Vista SP2's usbport.inf. The Vista reference was read statically on
; 2026-09-20 and then read AGAIN the same day as runtime, out of the running
; guest's own C:\Windows\inf\usbport.inf, which settles the one reference this
; file used to flag as uncertain. Windows 7's own copy has still NOT been read.
;
; Roadmap task 23.1.5 read it on FIVE guests, covering both install paths that
; carry this section and both architectures:
;
;   Windows 2000 SP4   NT 5.0  [Xhci.Dev.NTx86]    tab APPEARS
;   Windows XP SP3     NT 5.1  [Xhci.Dev.NTx86]    tab APPEARS
;   Windows XP x64     NT 5.2  [Xhci.Dev.NTamd64]  tab APPEARS  (amd64 file)
;   Windows Vista SP2  NT 6.0  [Xhci.Dev6.NTx86]   tab APPEARS
;   Windows 7 x86      NT 6.1  [Xhci.Dev6.NTx86]   tab APPEARS
;
; The page carries a bandwidth list, a Refresh button and an error-detection
; checkbox that writes ErrorCheckingEnabled 0/1 under HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\
; Windows\CurrentVersion\Usb, exactly as the 9x half's does. The baseline
; figure differs by generation and nothing here depends on it: NT 5.x reads
; "System reserved 10%", NT 6.x reads 20%.
;
; **THE ONE TRAP, AND IT COST THIS TASK A WHOLE FALSE NEGATIVE.** The roadmap's
; method was to write the pair BY HAND in regedit first and only then touch
; this file, the way the 9x line was taken in 2026-09-07. On Windows 2000 and
; Windows XP that works - the tab appears the moment the property sheet is
; re-opened. ON VISTA IT DOES NOT. A hand-written EnumPropPages32 on an
; already-installed devnode produced NO tab at all: not on re-opening the
; sheet, not with Device Manager restarted, not after a full reboot, while
; Microsoft's own EHCI controller on the same guest and boot showed its page
; from the identical pair. The SAME pair, written by THIS FILE at install
; time, gives Vista the tab. So on NT 6.x the provider list is consulted when
; the devnode is built and a value added afterwards is never read.
;
; Do not "verify" this line by adding it to a devnode by hand on NT 6.x, and
; do not read such a result as the line failing. Re-install the package.
; docs\contributing\lessons.md carries this; runs\run-23.md leg V has the
; readings on both sides of it.
;
; usbui.dll is the provider here and sysclass.dll is NOT: sysclass.dll is
; 16-bit and 9x-only. This INF already copies usbui.dll on the NT 5.x paths
; (Xhci.CopyUI) and NT 6.x ships it in System32, so the provider is present on
; every NT path this package installs on.
;
; EnumPropPages32 ALONE IS LOAD-BEARING. With Controller deleted and
; EnumPropPages32 left in place, the Windows 2000 tab still rendered and still
; read its bandwidth figure live (23.1.5, leg W8). Both lines ship because all
; three references write both and this file follows them, not because the pair
; is indivisible.
;
; No flags field on EnumPropPages32: FLG_ADDREG_TYPE_SZ is 0 and is spelled as
; an EMPTY field, which is how all three references write it. Controller is
; REG_BINARY, flags 1, one byte 01.

[Xhci.AddReg.NT]
HKR,,XhciLogVerbosity,0x00010001,0
HKR,,XhciLogDebugView,0x00010001,0
HKR,,EnumPropPages32,,"usbui.dll,USBControllerPropPageProvider"
HKR,,Controller,1,01

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Windows 2000 SP4 (.NTx86 - most specific match wins on NT)
; ------------------------------------------------------------------

[Xhci.Dev.NTx86]
AddReg=Xhci.AddReg.NT
CopyFiles=Xhci.CopyFiles,Xhci.CopyNT,Xhci.CopyUI

[Xhci.Dev.NTx86.Services]
AddService=xhci98,0x00000002,Xhci.AddService

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Windows Vista and Windows 7, 32-bit (.NTx86 of the NT 6.x models section)
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
;
; The same registry values and the same service, and xhci98.sys alone. None of
; the four OS-supplied files is on this path: the NT 6.x file queue resolves a
; source for each BEFORE COPYFLG_NO_OVERWRITE can skip it, finds the OS's own
; driver-store package, and aborts (task 21.8, Vista x64); and all four are on
; disk after any install of either system, with no USB host controller at all
; (task 22.3, read off the 32-bit guests). This shape installed on both 32-bit
; NT 6.x guests on 2026-09-13 from a staged copy. OS-ONNT6 in the INF gate
; refuses an OS file here.
;
; No right-click route of its own, and the right-click Install is not supported
; on NT 6.x: [DefaultInstall.NTx86] is the one that engine runs, and its
; LayoutFile copies have no source there. Read on Vista x86 (roadmap 22.5,
; 2026-09-17): it asks for usbport.sys from "(Unknown)", a Cancel aborts the
; queue with no message, and xhci98.sys is left in System32\drivers with no
; service. Use the device install.
[Xhci.Dev6.NTx86]
AddReg=Xhci.AddReg.NT
CopyFiles=Xhci.CopyFiles

[Xhci.Dev6.NTx86.Services]
AddService=xhci98,0x00000002,Xhci.AddService

[Xhci.AddService]
DisplayName=%XhciSvcDesc%
ServiceType=1                       ; SERVICE_KERNEL_DRIVER
StartType=3                         ; SERVICE_DEMAND_START
ErrorControl=1                      ; SERVICE_ERROR_NORMAL
ServiceBinary=%12%\xhci98.sys
LoadOrderGroup=Base

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Shared file lists
; ------------------------------------------------------------------

; The third field is the temporary name. Reinstalling over a loaded xhci98.sys
; is the normal case in the deploy loop; without it Win98 has no way to replace
; the file and can leave the previous binary in place. Win98 lists xhci98.tmp
; in the device's Driver File Details whether or not the file exists (the list
; comes from this entry, not from the disk), and an upgrade can leave the .tmp
; behind in System32\Drivers. Both are cosmetic; confirm which binary is live
; from the trace's "DriverEntry (built ...)" line.
[Xhci.CopyFiles]
xhci98.sys,,xhci98.tmp

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The files the OS supplies: usbd.sys and usbhub.sys on both targets,
; usbport.sys on the NT targets (usbui.dll follows, in its own block)
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
;
; None of these three is in [SourceDisksFiles], so each is resolved through
; LayoutFile (see [Version]) and fetched from the OS's own install source
; under its own name: each target gets its own OS's build by construction,
; and the media carries no Microsoft file. The one flag is 16 =
; COPYFLG_NO_OVERWRITE, "do not copy if file exists on target"
; (SETUPAPI.H). Presence is the entire requirement (an import has to
; resolve), so an existing file is left strictly alone whatever its version:
; a machine that ever had a USB controller Windows recognised already has
; all of them, and is asked for nothing. That is deliberately stronger than
; the version-based flags: 4 (COPYFLG_NOVERSIONCHECK) would overwrite a newer
; hotfix build, and 32 (COPYFLG_NO_VERSION_DIALOG) still replaces an older
; one for no benefit. The reference INFs use decimal in this field, so this
; does too.
;
; What the user sees on an xHCI-only Windows 98 machine is the engine's own
; "Insert Disk" prompt naming the Windows 98 CD, unless the CABs are on the
; hard disk (OEM and Windows 98 QuickInstall installs), in which case nothing
; is asked. The NT targets take the files from Driver Cache\i386, which every
; install has, and ask for nothing. Measured 2026-09-02 in the VMs for
; usbd.sys; docs\using\release-notes.md states it for the user.
;
; usbhub.sys is Windows 98's composite parent driver: USB.INF binds a
; multi-interface device to it, and without it every composite device on a
; NUSB machine stops at "USB Composite Device" with Code 2 (matched but
; unloadable). Under SweetLow's stack composites are parented by its
; usbccgp.sys instead, and this copy is inert there. On the NT targets the
; same name is the OS's own hub driver, and it is delivered there too since
; 1.0.1.0: it is not "placed by every install" as this file said until then.
; Both NT targets' layout.inf give usbport.sys, usbhub.sys, usbehci.sys and
; usbd.sys the text-mode disposition that does not copy them at Setup, so
; an NT install that never had a USB controller has none of them (read from
; the CDs on 2026-09-03; the Windows XP guest installed that day had exactly
; that set missing, and the Phase 2d Windows 2000 listing shows usbhub20.sys
; and no usbhub.sys).
;
; usbport.sys is the one that stops the driver loading at all: xhci98.sys
; imports it, and an unresolved import is Code 39 with nothing written to
; the trace (the Windows XP reading). The NT targets keep it in the same
; driver cache (XP SP3 in sp3.cab, Windows 2000 SP4 in sp4.cab, beside
; driver.cab), and layout.inf records that, so the same directive fetches
; it. It is NOT on the Windows 98 path: Windows 98's layout.inf has no such
; file, so the engine could not resolve it, and NUSB or SweetLow's stack
; supplies it there.
;
; usbhub20.sys, Windows 2000's driver for the USB 2.0 root hub usbport
; creates for this controller, is deliberately NOT here, on either path.
; Windows 2000 SP4's own USB.INF binds USB\ROOT_HUB20 to it and its
; [ROOTHUB2.NT] section copies usbhub20.sys from the driver cache when that
; PDO appears (read from the SP4 CD on 2026-09-03; it is how every Phase 2
; image got the file), so the OS places it itself; XP has no such file at
; all (its usbport.inf binds ROOT_HUB20 to usbhub.sys and copies usbhub.sys
; and usbd.sys with it), and a row XP's layout.inf cannot resolve has no
; source on XP. The owner's decision of 2026-09-03: leave it out, and read
; the root hub coming up on clean guests of both NT targets (roadmap tasks
; 19.4 and 19.5).
;
; scripts\inf-gate\check-inf.ps1 enforces all of it: LayoutFile present,
; none of the three in [SourceDisksFiles], each path copying usbd.sys and
; usbhub.sys with flag 16 to 10,System32\Drivers, usbport.sys on the NT
; path and not on the Windows 98 one, and usbhub20.sys on neither.

[Xhci.CopyW98]
usbd.sys,,,16
usbhub.sys,,,16

[Xhci.CopyNT]
usbport.sys,,,16
usbd.sys,,,16
usbhub.sys,,,16

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; usbui.dll - the property-page DLL, dirid 11, all four paths
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
;
; The same LayoutFile route and the same flag 16 as the three files above,
; but a different destination (dirid 11, not System32\Drivers) and a
; different reason, so it gets its own section. All four target CDs carry
; it and all four layout.inf files have a row for it, read statically on
; 2026-09-07: Windows 98 SE usbui.dll=5,,147456 (BASE5.CAB, 4.10.2222),
; Windows ME =2,,147456 (BASE2.CAB, 4.90.3000), Windows 2000 SP4
; = 1,,59664 (disk 1, I386\DRIVER.CAB, 5.00.2134.1) and Windows XP SP3
; = 100,,74240 (the SP source, I386\SP3.CAB, 5.1.2600.5512). Four distinct
; per-OS builds, each fetched from its own OS by construction.
;
; WHY, and it is an NT reason, not a 9x one. usbui.dll is the NT targets'
; USB property-page provider, and the OS's own INF has ALREADY registered
; it against the root hub this driver's usbport creates: Windows 2000's
; USB.INF [ROOTHUB2.NT] and Windows XP's usbport.inf [ROOTHUB.Dev.NT] both
; write HKR,,EnumPropPages32,,"usbui.dll,USBHubPropPageProvider". On an
; xHCI-only machine that file was never placed, so the reference dangles
; and Windows drops the page silently. Measured 2026-09-07 in both NT
; guests: without the file the USB Root Hub has General and Driver only
; (plus XP's Details); with it copied in, a Power tab appears and renders
; "The hub is self-powered", "Total power available: 500 mA per port" and
; the attached-device list. No registry change from this package is
; involved - placing the file is the whole repair.
;
; On Windows 98 and Windows ME it buys no tab, and that was measured too
; rather than assumed (see [Xhci.AddReg] above): the 9x controller page
; comes from sysclass.dll, which this INF does not copy and every 9x
; machine already has, and it renders identically with usbui.dll renamed
; away. The file goes on the 9x paths anyway, by the owner's decision of
; 2026-09-07, because it mirrors what Windows 98 SE's and Windows ME's own
; USB.INF do (USBUI.CopyFiles=11) and because the same dirid-11 section
; then serves all four paths unchanged.
;
; Windows 2000 was the one prompt risk, and it is silent: its usbd.sys,
; usbhub.sys and usbport.sys come from disk 2 (sp4.cab) but usbui.dll from
; disk 1, the base CD, satisfied out of driver.cab. Both cabs sit in
; Driver Cache\i386 on every install. Read on 2026-09-07 on an xHCI-only
; Windows 2000 guest holding none of the four files: it fetched three from
; sp4.cab and usbui.dll from driver.cab in one pass and asked for nothing. On
; Windows XP it is free (sp3.cab, the same cab as usbport.sys and
; usbhub.sys), and on 9x it rides the Insert Disk prompt the install
; already raises (the same BASE cab as usbd.sys and usbhub.sys).
[Xhci.CopyUI]
usbui.dll,,,16

[Inf.CopyFiles]
xhci98.inf

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Right-click Install - pre-stages the files with no device present
; ------------------------------------------------------------------

[DefaultInstall]
CopyFiles=Inf.CopyFiles,Xhci.CopyFiles,Xhci.CopyW98,Xhci.CopyUI

; Win2000's setup engine keeps its own copy of an OEM INF as oemN.inf; copying
; this one into %17% as well would leave two INFs claiming PCI\CC_0C0330.
[DefaultInstall.NTx86]
CopyFiles=Xhci.CopyFiles,Xhci.CopyNT,Xhci.CopyUI

; ASCII only, and that is a parser requirement rather than a style note:
; [Strings] is read by both engines and this file is ANSI/CRLF for Win98's
; 16-bit one, so a curly quote or an en dash here makes that engine report
; nothing at all (docs\contributing\build-and-test.md, "Win98 INF-parser
; traps").
;
; %XhciDesc% is what Device Manager shows, what the "Have Disk" list shows
; during the documented install, and what a user reads back in a bug report;
; on a machine that also has EHCI it appears beside Microsoft's own entries.
; The "(xhci98)" marker is therefore load-bearing. "USB 2.0" leads the string
; deliberately: this driver manages only the USB 2.0 protocol ports and leaves
; the SuperSpeed ones unpowered by design (AGENTS.md, "Port Strategy"), and a
; name that says only "eXtensible Host Controller" invites the reading that
; SuperSpeed devices will run at SuperSpeed. Keep it first, where it survives
; a truncated column.
;
; Changing these strings is not observable on an existing devnode: both
; engines cache DriverDesc in the device's software key at install time, so
; the observation is a fresh install, or an uninstall-and-reinstall, on each
; target.
[Strings]
Provider="Yeo Kheng Meng"
Mfg="xHCI98 Project"
DiskName="xhci98 xHCI Miniport Disk"
XhciDesc="USB 2.0 eXtensible Host Controller (xhci98)"
XhciSvcDesc="xHCI USB 2.0 Host Controller Miniport (xhci98)"
